course: dictionary
lesson: 8842
title: Shipyards: where ships are built
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A shipyard is a place by the water where ships are built and repaired. Shipyard is made of two smaller words, ship and yard, so the word tells you what happens there.
explain: «The Arsenal of Venice was a huge state shipyard that could build ships one after another, almost like a factory.» The clues are built, one after another and like a factory. So a shipyard is a busy place where many ships are made, not just one.
explain: Shipyards can be famous. In history lessons you may read about shipyards in Belfast, where the Titanic was built, and in Glasgow and Newcastle. Shipyard workers build ships for navies and companies, so the word often appears in the news too.
fact: A shipyard is a place by the water where ships are built and repaired.
fact: The word shipyard is made from ship and yard.
fact: Shipyard workers build ships for navies and companies.
fact: A naval shipyard builds and repairs ships for the navy.
fact: Plural: one shipyard, two shipyards.
exercise: Shipyard workers build ships for navies and companies. Who builds the ships? | *Shipyard workers | Sailors on holiday | Teachers | Fishermen
exercise: «The Arsenal of Venice was a huge state shipyard that could build ships one after another, almost like a factory.» Where were the ships built? | In a shop | In a field | *In a shipyard | In a school
exercise: A naval shipyard is a shipyard for the navy. What does naval mean? | *Connected with the navy | Connected with cooking | Very fast | Made of wood
test: In history lessons, shipyards in Belfast were famous for building the Titanic. Which city's shipyard built the Titanic? | Glasgow | Venice | *Belfast | Newcastle
test: Which is the plural of shipyard? | shipyardes | *shipyards | shipyard's | shipyard-s
exam: «The Arsenal of Venice was a huge state shipyard.» What does huge mean here? | *Very large | Very wet | Very small | Very old
